course: dictionary
lesson: 3366
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word «exclusion» is a good example. In a school sentence it means a pupil is sent away from school as a punishment. «A suspension (also called an exclusion) means a pupil must stay away from school for a set number of days.» The words pupil and school are the clues.
explain: Now read about a closed place: «Wildlife returned to the Chornobyl exclusion zone, the closed area around the plant.» The clues are closed area and around the plant. An exclusion zone is an area people must keep out of. Ask yourself what the sentence is about, then try your meaning in it to check.
explain: Exclusion can also mean leaving someone out. If a group of friends will not let one child join their game, that is exclusion, and it can hurt. The same word can mean keeping out, being sent away, or leaving out. Context always tells you which one.
fact: Exclusion is a noun. It means keeping someone or something out, or leaving them out.
fact: In UK schools, exclusion means a pupil is sent away from school for a time as a punishment.
fact: «Exclusion zone» means a closed area that people must keep out of.
fact: You can say «an exclusion» for one punishment, and «exclusions» for more than one.
fact: When a word has more than one meaning, look at the words around it to find the right meaning.
exercise: A suspension (also called an exclusion) means a pupil must stay away from school for a set number of days. In this sentence, what does exclusion mean? | A school trip to a new place | A prize for good work | *Being sent away from school for a set number of days | Learning a new language
exercise: Wildlife returned to the Chornobyl exclusion zone, the closed area around the plant. What is an exclusion zone? | A school for wild animals | A zone for swimming | *An area people must keep out of | A park where people can picnic
exercise: Maya's friends would not let her join their game at break time. What is this? | A suspension from school | *Exclusion, leaving her out | An exclusion zone near the playground | A policy that covers lost property
test: «The exclusion of girls from the team was unfair.» What does exclusion mean here? | *Keeping girls out of the team | Letting girls choose their own team | Asking girls to join a club | Giving girls extra training
test: «The insurance policy had an exclusion for lost phones.» What does exclusion mean here? | *Something not covered or included | A punishment at school | Leaving a game early | A closed area people must keep out of
exam: The head teacher said Ravi would face exclusion for a week after the fight. What does exclusion mean here? | Being asked to join a club | Something not included in a school trip | A closed area near the school | *Being sent away from school as a punishment
